`grid-template-areas` lets you draw the layout as ASCII art and place items by name instead of counting lines:

```css
.dashboard {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 60px 1fr 40px;
  grid-template-areas:
    "sidebar header"
    "sidebar content"
    "sidebar footer";
}
.header  { grid-area: header; }
.content { grid-area: content; }
.footer  { grid-area: footer; }
.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
```

The string literal has to be a rectangle — every row needs the same number of cells, and a named area must form a single contiguous rectangle, not an L-shape. That constraint is a feature: the CSS itself reads as a literal picture of the layout, which is far easier for a teammate to review than a wall of `grid-column: 2 / 4; grid-row: 1 / 3;` line-number math.

This single declaration produces a genuinely responsive card grid: more columns on wide viewports, fewer on narrow ones, no breakpoints written by hand.

```css
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  gap: 16px;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
}
```

`auto-fit` computes how many 220px-minimum columns fit in the container, then stretches them to share whatever width remains — so at 900px wide you might get four columns; at 400px, one. Swap `auto-fit` for `auto-fill` and the difference only shows when there are *fewer items than columns that would fit*: `auto-fill` keeps the empty tracks (so existing cards stay their minmax size rather than stretching to fill the row), while `auto-fit` collapses empty tracks to zero width and lets the real cards expand into that space.
